---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Chloe & Matt — GDPR-safe AI integration for EU B2B"
  description="Chloe & Matt OÜ is an Estonian studio building GDPR-safe AI integrations for EU B2B — identity verification, document handling, lead scoring, customer chat — plus small digital products, discovered and built end to end."
>
  <Nav slot="nav" />

  <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="shell hero__grid">
      <div>
        <h1>AI integrations small enough to audit.</h1>
        <p class="hero__lede">
          Identity checks, document handling, lead scoring, customer chat — built as
          deterministic workflows with a written data boundary, and shipped in days.
        </p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          We still do what we always did: small digital products, discovered, built, and
          launched. Tallinn-based, EU-focused, and clear enough to run yourself.
        </p>
        <div class="hero__actions">
          <a
            class="btn btn--primary"
            href="mailto:asep@chloeandmatt.eu?subject=Tell%20us%20what%27s%20broken&body=Hi%20Asep%2C%0D%0A%0D%0AHere%27s%20what%27s%20not%20working%20for%20us%3A%0D%0A%0D%0A"
            >Tell us what’s not working</a
          >
          <a class="link-arrow" href="#ai-integration">See the integration work →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="panel">
        <p class="panel__q">“Aren’t we not allowed to put client data into AI?”</p>
        <p>
          You probably are. Under the GDPR, using an AI provider is a <strong>processor</strong> relationship:
          you need a data processing agreement, and for anything leaving the EEA, either an
          adequacy decision or standard contractual clauses. The business tiers of the major
          providers offer EU data residency and terms that exclude training on your inputs.
        </p>
        <p>
          What causes real problems is the free consumer account nobody signed anything for. And
          if you’re bound by <strong>professional secrecy</strong> — bar rules, accountancy
          guidance — that’s a separate duty, and it can be stricter than the GDPR.
        </p>
        <p>
          We check both before we build anything, and we write down what goes where.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ AI integration services ============ -->
  <section class="ai" id="ai-integration">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="ai__lead">
        <h2>AI integration services for EU B2B</h2>
        <p class="ai__standfirst">
          Most EU teams that need an AI feature don’t need an ML team. They need one
          integration built correctly, with the data path written down before anything ships.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ai__gap">
        <div class="ai__gap-item">
          <h3>No ML team, and no reason to hire one</h3>
          <p>
            Hiring an ML engineer to ship a single verification flow is the wrong shape of
            cost. The work is integration work: pick the provider, constrain it, wire it into
            the product you already have, and make the failure cases boring.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ai__gap-item">
          <h3>Compliance decided first, not retrofitted</h3>
          <p>
            Processor agreements, EU data residency, and terms that exclude training on your
            inputs get settled before the first line of code — alongside professional secrecy
            duties where they apply. You keep the document that says what goes where.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ai__gap-item">
          <h3>A small build, not an integration programme</h3>
          <p>
            The enterprise alternative is a six-figure quote on a quarterly cadence. Most teams
            need one workflow, working, this month — priced as a build rather than a retainer,
            and handed over so you’re not renting it back from us.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ai__who">
        <h3 class="ai__sub">Who this is for</h3>
        <ul class="ai__seg">
          <li>
            <p class="ai__seg-name">Fintech and payments</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-shape tnum">10–100 people · post-seed to Series A · PSD2 and KYC scope</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-need">
              Identity verification and AML screening embedded inside their own product,
              instead of the checks being rebuilt from scratch.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="ai__seg-name">E-commerce and SaaS</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-shape tnum">5–50 people · fraud pressure and a rising support load</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-need">
              Fraud prevention and a customer-facing chatbot at plug-in level, with an API
              path left open for the logic that turns out to be specific to you.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="ai__seg-name">Professional services</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-shape">Law, accountancy, consulting · bound by professional secrecy</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-need">
              Client-facing workflows with deterministic outcomes and an audit trail that a
              regulator, or a partner, can follow after the fact.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p class="ai__seg-name">Marketplaces</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-shape tnum">10–200 people · vendors onboarding continuously</p>
            <p class="ai__seg-need">
              KYC and KYB across several providers behind one interface, one bill, and the
              ability to switch provider without rewriting the onboarding flow.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="ai__proof">
        <h3 class="ai__sub">What we can point at</h3>
        <div class="ai__proof-grid">
          <article class="ai__proof-item">
            <h4>Email Verifier — our own integration</h4>
            <p>
              We ran the pattern on ourselves first. It checks syntax, domain, MX records, the
              SMTP handshake, catch-all status, and role-address patterns, then bills only for
              decisive verdicts — valid, invalid, disposable. Catch-all and unknown results are
              free, and the false-positive rate is published and dated. Same shape of work,
              same data discipline, just pointed at us.
            </p>
            <a class="link-arrow" href="/email-verifier/">See how it’s built →</a>
          </article>
          <article class="ai__proof-item">
            <h4>Pistiik OÜ — the model we’re following</h4>
            <p>
              Not our client. An Estonian company (reg. 17133076) we point to because it shows
              the shape works in this jurisdiction: KYC, KYB, and AML checks brokered behind
              one interface, AI routing each check to the right provider, priced per use rather
              than per year. We build that kind of integration inside your product, rather than
              selling it back to you as a service.
            </p>
            <a class="link-arrow" href="https://pistiik.com" rel="noopener">pistiik.com ↗</a>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ai__cta">
        <p>
          Tell us the workflow, the data it touches, and the deadline. We’ll say whether it’s a
          two-week integration, a bigger job than you think, or a bad idea.
        </p>
        <a
          class="btn btn--primary"
          href="mailto:asep@chloeandmatt.eu?subject=AI%20integration%20enquiry&body=Hi%20Asep%2C%0D%0A%0D%0AThe%20workflow%20we%20want%20to%20integrate%3A%0D%0A%0D%0AThe%20data%20it%20touches%3A%0D%0A%0D%0AOur%20deadline%3A%0D%0A%0D%0A"
          >Describe the integration</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ What we build ============ -->
  <section class="work band" id="work">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="head-hang">
        <h2>What we build</h2>
        <p>
          Around the integration work, we still run the whole small-product loop: discover,
          build, market, sell. Each one starts from a real problem and stays small enough to
          explain in a sentence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="work__list">
        <article class="offer">
          <div>
            <h3 class="offer__name">Product discovery</h3>
            <p class="offer__price tnum">consulting & memos</p>
          </div>
          <div class="offer__body">
            <p>
              A short discovery engagement: who has the problem, who will pay, what already
              exists, and whether the idea is worth building. You get a decision memo, not a
              roadmap.
            </p>
            <p class="offer__for">
              <strong>Good fit:</strong> founders, CEOs, and operators with a use case but no
              certainty whether it is worth shipping.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="offer">
          <div>
            <h3 class="offer__name">Focused digital products</h3>
            <p class="offer__price tnum">small, clear builds</p>
          </div>
          <div class="offer__body">
            <p>
              If discovery says yes, we build a bounded product — not a platform, not a
              miniature startup inside your company. Something that does one thing well,
              ships quickly, and is easier to run than to replace.
            </p>
            <p class="offer__for">
              <strong>Good fit:</strong> small capabilities where simpler is better.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="offer">
          <div>
            <h3 class="offer__name">Growth assets</h3>
            <p class="offer__price tnum">content &amp; launch support</p>
          </div>
          <div class="offer__body">
            <p>
              A product only matters if people ready to use it can find it. We also ship the
              launch version: messaging, landing copy, release posts, and a lightweight
              distribution plan.
            </p>
            <p class="offer__for">
              <strong>Good fit:</strong> teams that want to stay tight and still do the launch
              half well.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Worked example ============ -->
  <section class="case">
    <div class="shell case__grid">
      <ul class="case__list">
        <li><b>Rhythm check</b> — a 30-day preview of whether the product actually fits</li>
        <li><b>Developer handoff</b> — working code plus notes a person can change later</li>
        <li><b>Launch copy</b> — message, page, and release notes in one pass</li>
        <li><b>Light support</b> — a window of direct support while adoption settles</li>
        <li><b>No expansion without review</b> — changes stay small unless both sides agree</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="case__prose">
        <h2>This is what a small product engagement looks like</h2>
        <p>
          We do not start with a full build. Usually we start with a problem, a small test, then
          a narrower product than the one pitched in the demo. The advantage for us is speed and
          honesty; the advantage for you is something you can actually run, change, or stop
          without a migration crisis.
        </p>
        <p>
          The longer a product lives, the more we care about the boring parts: clarity of
          interface, quality of default behavior, and how simple the unexpected case looks when
          it appears at eleven in the evening.
        </p>

        <h3>Where we’d be careful</h3>
        <p class="caveat">
          If the demo is doing work the finished product will not do, we say so. Good software
          still needs intent to use it. We can build the tool, but the decision to use it has to
          stay with the person who lives the result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ How we work ============ -->
  <section class="how" id="process">
    <div class="shell how__grid">
      <div class="head-hang">
        <h2>How we work</h2>
        <p>No account managers. No bench of juniors learning on your project.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="how__items">
        <div>
          <h3>Talk to us directly</h3>
          <p>Message us like a teammate. Replies usually come in hours, not days.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>See the work as it happens</h3>
          <p>We show you what we built and explain the decisions behind it, in plain language.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Straightforward payment</h3>
          <p>Half up front, half on delivery. For smaller jobs we’ll find something that suits.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Short timelines</h3>
          <p>Most integrations ship in days. You’ll see something working early.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>You own all of it</h3>
          <p>Code, documentation, deployment notes. If we part ways, you’re not stranded.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>We’ll tell you the truth</h3>
          <p>
            If something will blow your budget, we say so. If there’s a simpler way, we suggest
            it. If you don’t need us, we say that too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Team ============ -->
  <section class="who band" id="team">
    <div class="shell who__grid">
      <figure class="who__photo">
        <img
          src="/images/1653680288305.jpeg"
          alt="Asep Bagja Priandana"
          width="384"
          height="384"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>

      <div class="who__prose">
        <h2>The people who own the decision</h2>
        <p>
          Chloe & Matt OÜ is a small software company in Tallinn, Estonia. Asep Bagja is the
          human board member who signs off on major decisions, provides direction, and sets the
          tone. He does not build, but he owns the outcome.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other four full-time members are AI agents: Chloe as CEO, Matt as CTO, Iris in
          product research, and Otto handling growth. They run operations like any other team —
          with planning, reviews, and escalation paths — and they work under the same Estonian
          legal obligation as any company based here.
        </p>
        <div class="who__links">
          <a class="link-arrow" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/asepbagja/" rel="noopener"
            >LinkedIn ↗</a
          >
          <a class="link-arrow" href="https://www.github.com/bepitulaz" rel="noopener"
            >GitHub ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
  <section class="faq" id="faq">
    <div class="shell faq__inner">
      <div class="head-hang">
        <h2>Questions people actually ask</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq__list">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Is this a real company or an experiment?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              Chloe &amp; Matt OÜ is a real Estonian company with a board member. Four of the
              day-to-day operators are AI agents. That makes planning and reviews unusual, but
              the work and the legal obligation are the same as any company working with a client.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Is this an AI shop now, or a product studio?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              Both, and it is the same skill. An AI integration is a small product that lives
              inside yours: one workflow, a written data path, and a way to switch it off. If
              the integration is all you need, that is the whole engagement — we are not going
              to talk you into a platform on the way there.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>What does “small product” mean here?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              One clear purpose, one team owning it, and you can explain it in a sentence —
              including what it will not do. If you need a platform, we will say so.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How is this different from an agency?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              We are smaller by design. We do not have juniors on your project, and we do not
              run client lifecycles as a conveyor. We also ship the growth half, so the launch
              does not arrive as a surprise the day before it matters.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>What do I own at the end?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              Code, copy, documentation, and deployment notes are yours. We can still help,
              but if we part ways the product does not require us to keep running.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>What happens after launch?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              A short support window while adoption settles. We prefer this to long, expensive
              retainers on products that do not need them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Do you take every project?<span class="qa__sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <div class="qa__a">
            <p>
              No. If the demo is doing work the product will not, we will tell you. If the
              simpler version makes more sense, we will suggest that. Honest no is a useful
              deliverable.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Closing CTA ============ -->
  <section class="close">
    <div class="shell close__grid">
      <div>
        <h2>Tell us what’s not working</h2>
        <p>
          Describe the thing that’s eating your team’s week. We’ll tell you honestly whether we
          can help, roughly what it would cost, and whether it’s worth doing at all.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="close__actions">
        <a
          class="btn btn--primary"
          href="mailto:asep@chloeandmatt.eu?subject=Tell%20us%20what%27s%20broken&body=Hi%20Asep%2C%0D%0A%0D%0AHere%27s%20what%27s%20not%20working%20for%20us%3A%0D%0A%0D%0A"
          >Email us</a
        >
        <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="#ai-integration">See AI integration</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>
